<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Workingmen's Association</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as First International, International Working Men's Association</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Workingmen's Association, commonly known as the First International, was a political international that aimed to unite left-wing groups and trade unions of the working class. It was founded at a meeting of some 2,000 workers in St Martin's Hall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 28 September 1864, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> drafted its Inaugural Address and Provisional Rules. Conflict between Marx's supporters and the anarchists around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a> led to a split at the Hague Congress of 1872, which expelled Bakunin and moved the General Council to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. The International was formally disbanded in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association.jpg" alt="International Workingmen's Association" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:International-founding-1864.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1844 the Fraternal Democrats were founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by political refugees and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartist</a> leaders, and the later International took their structure as its model. A new generation of British trade union leaders, known as the Junta, organised solidarity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> against Russian repression and with the Union side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. Contacts between British and French workers' leaders at the London International Exhibition of 1862 led to a meeting in London in July 1863.</p><p>The founding meeting was held at St Martin's Hall on 28 September 1864, with some 2,000 workers, and resolved to found an international organisation with a General Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. A committee of twenty-one was elected to draft its rules, including George Odger, the French delegates Henri Tolain and Eugène Varlin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>. Marx drafted the Inaugural Address and the Provisional Rules, after earlier drafts by Mazzinists and Owenites were rejected. The Preamble stated that the emancipation of the working classes must be achieved by the workers themselves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Association brought together socialists, communists and anarchists, and its history was one of factional struggle. The French members were at first dominated by the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a>, who opposed political action, strikes and collective ownership. They were largely defeated at the Geneva Congress of 1866, and the Brussels Congress of 1868 declared that mines, railways and arable land should become common property. From 1868 the Russian Mikhail Bakunin led an anti-statist opposition that wanted the state abolished.</p><p>The International reached its peak after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> of 1871, in which around a third of the members of the Commune's council belonged to it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> defended the Commune in The Civil War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but its suppression brought harsh repression, including a ban on the International in France. Modern research puts membership at its peak at around 150,000. At the Hague Congress in September 1872, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Bakunin</a> and James Guillaume were expelled, and on a proposal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> the General Council's seat was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. The anarchist wing formed a rival International at St. Imier, which disintegrated by 1877. The Association was officially disbanded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on 15 July 1876.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The First International did not achieve a social revolution, but it was the first mass international movement of the working class. It established the foundations of revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, moderate socialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a> as the three trends of the workers' movement, and its experience prepared the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-international">Second International</a> of 1889.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First International” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>International Workingmen’s Association</b>, the First International, was founded at St Martin’s Hall in London on 28 September 1864; divided between the followers of Marx and the anarchists around Mikhail Bakunin, it split in 1872 and was dissolved in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Mikhail Bakunin entered the <b>International Workingmen’s Association</b>, formed in 1864, where his faction and Proudhon’s mutualists opposed state socialism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In the last third of the 19th century democratic socialist parties, drawing mainly on Marxism, arose across Europe, while the split between authoritarian and libertarian socialists went back to the <b>First International</b> and its expulsion of the anarchists in 1872.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>It revived in the early 1870s, which Hobsbawm attributes to three causes: Marx's leadership of the <b>First International</b>, his support for the Paris Commune of 1871, and the treason trial of leaders of the Social Democratic Workers' Party of Germany, at which prosecutors read the text into the…</blockquote><a href="/work/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1934, at the request of the theatre director Erwin Piscator, Brecht wrote the song for the <b>First International</b> Workers' Music Olympiad, held in Strasbourg in 1935, where a choir of 3,000 workers gave its first performance.</blockquote><a href="/songs/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied</a></li><li><blockquote>Eugène Pottier, a member of the <b>First International</b> who had attended its congress of 1864 and a member of the Paris Commune, wrote the French words in June 1871, after the French army crushed the Commune on 28 May and before he fled to Britain and then, from…</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a></li></ul></section>
